items.docMarkdown:
Encodes a permutation of `0..N` (`N <= 32`) into a single 118-bit Lehmer factoradic `u128`.

Uses a 32-bit bitmask (`seen_mask`) and hardware population count (`u32::count_ones`) to map
`perm` bijectively onto `0..N!` in `O(N)` register operations—matching the mixed-radix wire
layout of `encodeBenesFactoradicBlock` in `lib/dbrain-core.js`.

# Arguments

* `perm` - Slice containing a permutation of `0..(perm.len() as u32)` with `perm.len() <= 32`.

# Returns

Mixed-radix factoradic rank in `0..(N!)` packed into a `u128` (`0` for reverse-sorted
`[N-1, ..., 0]`, and `N! - 1` for ascending `[0, 1, ..., N-1]`).

# Complexity

`O(N)` time (`N` hardware `POPCNT` and `u128` multiply-add steps) and `O(1)` register space.

# Examples

```rust
use dbrain_core_rs::benes::{decode_benes_factoradic_block, encode_benes_factoradic_block};

// Descending [3, 2, 1, 0] has 0 smaller previously-seen items at every step -> rank 0:
assert_eq!(encode_benes_factoradic_block(&[3, 2, 1, 0]), 0);

// Ascending [0, 1, 2, 3] hits the maximum digit j at every step -> 4! - 1 = 23:
assert_eq!(encode_benes_factoradic_block(&[0, 1, 2, 3]), 23);

// Arbitrary permutation round-trip:
let perm = [2u32, 0, 3, 1];
let rank = encode_benes_factoradic_block(&perm);
let mut decoded = [0u32; 4];
decode_benes_factoradic_block(rank, 4, &mut decoded);
assert_eq!(decoded, perm);
```

